---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RevealText from '../components/RevealText.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { link, repository } from '../lib/site';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Project principles — ZeroCloud"
  description="What zero required cloud dependence means for accounts, data, and connections between your devices."
>
  <div class="frame">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ text: 'Home', href: link() }, { text: 'Project' }]} />
    <header class="page-lead">
      <p class="eyeline" data-enter>The project</p>
      <h1 data-enter><RevealText text="What “local” means." /></h1>
      <p data-enter>
        “Zero” stands for zero required cloud services. A device can still connect to another
        device, run a local service, or download an update.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 data-enter><RevealText text="When the internet goes out" /></h2>
      <p data-enter>
        Two devices on the same reachable network can keep communicating even when that network
        loses internet access. ZeroCloud is built around that distinction. Essential workflows
        should continue on hardware the user controls.
      </p>
      <h2 data-enter><RevealText text="Who holds the data" /></h2>
      <p data-enter>
        The design calls for processing and storage on the device. When you choose to share
        something, the intended destination is the device you selected. Finding a peer should also
        be possible locally.
      </p>
      <h2 data-enter><RevealText text="Where the project stands" /></h2>
      <p data-enter>
        ZeroCloudShare is the first app. Its repository contains a desktop shell, an Android shell,
        and their shared interface. The team has yet to implement pairing or file transport.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={repository}>Read the project repository ↗</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
